{
 "title": "Why You Can't Tickle Yourself",
 "scenes": [
  {
   "id": "short",
   "tail": 0.2,
   "beats": [
    {
     "id": "b00",
     "say": "Try to tickle yourself."
    },
    {
     "id": "b01",
     "say": "Right now."
    },
    {
     "id": "b02",
     "say": "Go on."
    },
    {
     "id": "b03",
     "say": "Nothing, right?"
    },
    {
     "id": "b04",
     "say": "There's a reason."
    },
    {
     "id": "b05",
     "say": "In the late nineteen nineties, neuroscientist Sarah-Jayne Blakemore and her team built a tickle robot."
    },
    {
     "id": "b06",
     "say": "People moved a handle, and the robot stroked their palm with soft foam."
    },
    {
     "id": "b07",
     "say": "When it copied their movement instantly, it barely tickled."
    },
    {
     "id": "b08",
     "say": "Then they added a tiny delay."
    },
    {
     "id": "b09",
     "say": "A fifth of a second."
    },
    {
     "id": "b10",
     "say": "Suddenly, it tickled more."
    },
    {
     "id": "b11",
     "say": "Your brain predicts every move you make."
    },
    {
     "id": "b12",
     "say": "When it sees a touch coming, it turns the feeling down."
    },
    {
     "id": "b13",
     "say": "Your own hand can't surprise you."
    },
    {
     "id": "b14",
     "say": "And a tickle without surprise is just a touch."
    },
    {
     "id": "b15",
     "say": "Bet you're about to try it again."
    }
   ],
